D={
"minoan-civilisation":dict(government="Palace centres such as Knossos, Phaistos and Malia redistributed goods and organised religion. Rulers may have been priest-kings or queens, and frescoes show women prominently.",architecture="The palace of Knossos had multi-storey wings around a central court, light wells, painted plaster and flush drainage.",technology="Linear A script (still undeciphered), sophisticated ships and fine pottery such as Kamares ware.",rise="Bronze Age Cretan farming and sea trade grew into palace societies around 2000 BCE.",fall="The Thera (Santorini) eruption around 1600 BCE weakened them; by about 1450 BCE Mycenaean Greeks controlled Knossos.",legacy="Europe's first palace civilisation; myths of the Minotaur and labyrinth."),
"mycenaean-greece":dict(government="Warrior kings (wanax) ruled palace states such as Mycenae, Pylos and Thebes, with scribes recording goods in Linear B, an early form of Greek.",architecture="Mycenae's Lion Gate, Cyclopean walls and the beehive tholos tomb called the Treasury of Atreus.",technology="Bronze armour (the Dendra panoply), chariots and Linear B.",rise="Mainland Greeks absorbed Minoan culture from about 1600 BCE.",fall="The palaces were destroyed around 1200 BCE in the Bronze Age collapse. Causes probably combined drought, earthquakes, raiders and systems failure.",legacy="The world of Homer's Iliad and Odyssey."),
"classical-greece":dict(government="Hundreds of city-states (poleis) had different constitutions. Athens built a direct democracy in which citizen men voted in the Assembly and juries were chosen by lot after Cleisthenes' reforms (508 BCE). Sparta was a militarised oligarchy with two kings.",architecture="The Parthenon (447–432 BCE) with its Doric columns, theatres such as Epidaurus, and the agora and stoa.",technology="Mathematics (Pythagoras, Euclid), medicine (Hippocrates), the trireme warship, and the Antikythera mechanism (later Hellenistic).",rise="After the Greek Dark Ages, city-states revived from the 8th century BCE with the alphabet, colonies and the Olympic Games (776 BCE). They defeated Persia at Marathon (490), Salamis (480) and Plataea (479).",fall="The Peloponnesian War (431–404 BCE) between Athens and Sparta exhausted Greece, and Philip II of Macedon won control at Chaeronea (338 BCE).",legacy="Democracy, philosophy (Socrates, Plato, Aristotle), history (Herodotus, Thucydides), theatre and science."),
"macedonian-empire":dict(government="Alexander ruled as king of Macedon and as successor of the Persian Great King, keeping satrapies and mixing Macedonian and Persian officials.",architecture="Alexander founded over a dozen cities called Alexandria, and his successors built the Library and Lighthouse of Alexandria.",technology="The sarissa phalanx combined with heavy cavalry, plus siege towers and torsion catapults.",rise="Philip II reformed the army and unified Greece. Alexander (r. 336–323 BCE) conquered Persia, Egypt and lands to the Indus in 11 years.",fall="Alexander died in Babylon in 323 BCE at 32. His generals (the Diadochi) split the empire into the Ptolemaic, Seleucid and Antigonid kingdoms.",legacy="The Hellenistic age spread Greek language and culture from Egypt to India, including Gandhara art."),
"roman-republic":dict(government="Two consuls elected annually, a Senate of ex-magistrates, and assemblies of citizens. Tribunes protected plebeians, and laws were written on the Twelve Tables (451 BCE).",architecture="Roads such as the Via Appia (312 BCE), aqueducts, basilicas and the Forum.",technology="Roman concrete, legionary organisation and surveying.",rise="Romans expelled their last king in 509 BCE, conquered Italy, defeated Carthage and took Greece.",fall="Inequality, the Gracchi reforms and their murder, generals' private armies, civil wars from Marius and Sulla to Caesar, and Caesar's assassination (44 BCE). Octavian became Augustus in 27 BCE.",legacy="Checks and balances, the Senate and republican ideals that inspired the US and French founders."),
"byzantine-empire":dict(government="An autocratic Christian empire: the emperor was God's representative, aided by a large salaried bureaucracy. Justinian's Code (529–534) compiled Roman law.",architecture="Hagia Sophia (537), whose dome seemed to float on light, Ravenna's mosaics and the Theodosian Walls of Constantinople.",technology="Greek fire, a liquid incendiary weapon, defended the capital, which also had silk production and the gold solidus, a stable coin for 700 years.",rise="Constantine founded Constantinople in 330. When the Western Empire fell (476), the East continued as the Roman Empire.",fall="Defeats by Seljuks at Manzikert (1071), the Fourth Crusade's sack of Constantinople (1204), shrinking territory, and the Ottoman conquest of 1453.",legacy="Preserved Greek and Roman texts, spread Orthodox Christianity and the Cyrillic alphabet to Slavs."),
"celts":dict(government="Tribes led by chiefs and warrior aristocracies, with druids as priests, judges and teachers.",architecture="Hillforts, oppida (fortified towns) such as Bibracte, and crannogs (lake dwellings).",technology="Iron working, chain mail and fine La Tène metalwork.",rise="Hallstatt (from ~800 BCE) and La Tène (from ~450 BCE) cultures spread across Europe from Ireland to Anatolia.",fall="Roman conquest of Gaul (Caesar, 58–50 BCE) and Britain (43 CE); later Germanic migrations.",legacy="Irish, Scottish Gaelic, Welsh and Breton languages and Celtic art."),
"vikings":dict(government="Norse chieftains and kings ruled with the advice of the 'thing', an assembly of free men. Iceland's Althing (930) is one of the oldest parliaments.",architecture="Longhouses, ring fortresses such as Trelleborg, and stave churches.",technology="Clinker-built longships, navigation, and a runic alphabet.",rise="The raid on Lindisfarne (793) began the Viking Age. They settled in England, Ireland, Normandy, Iceland and Greenland, and reached Newfoundland (~1000).",fall="Christianisation and stronger kingdoms ended raiding by about 1066.",legacy="Normandy, Kievan Rus, the sagas and many English words."),
"holy-roman-empire":dict(government="An elected emperor (chosen by seven, later more, prince-electors) presided over hundreds of semi-independent states, cities and bishoprics, with an Imperial Diet (Reichstag).",architecture="Aachen Cathedral, Speyer Cathedral and Gothic cathedrals.",technology="Gutenberg's printing press at Mainz (~1450).",rise="Otto I was crowned emperor in 962, building on Charlemagne's title (800).",fall="The Reformation and the Thirty Years' War (1618–48) weakened it. Napoleon forced its dissolution in 1806.",legacy="German federalism, as Voltaire mocked: 'neither holy, nor Roman, nor an empire'."),
"kievan-rus":dict(government="Rurikid princes ruled Kiev and other cities with councils of nobles (boyars) and town assemblies (veche).",architecture="St Sophia's Cathedral in Kyiv and the Golden Gate.",technology="Trade along the Dnieper to Byzantium.",rise="Varangian (Viking) leader Rurik and his successor Oleg united eastern Slav lands in the late 9th century.",fall="Princely feuds and the Mongol destruction of Kyiv (1240).",legacy="Christianisation by Vladimir (988); shared heritage of Ukraine, Belarus and Russia."),
"spanish-empire":dict(government="The Habsburg monarchy ruled through councils (the Council of the Indies) and viceroyalties of New Spain and Peru.",architecture="Baroque cathedrals and colonial grid towns across the Americas, and El Escorial.",technology="Silver mining at Potosí using mercury amalgamation; Manila galleons crossing the Pacific.",rise="Columbus's voyage (1492), conquest of the Aztecs (1521) and Incas (1532).",fall="Wars, inflation, then independence movements (1810–1825) in Latin America and defeat by the US (1898).",legacy="Spanish is spoken by about 500 million people; the Columbian Exchange."),
"portuguese-empire":dict(government="Crown-sponsored trading posts (feitorias) and captaincies.",architecture="Forts in Goa, Malacca and Africa; the Manueline style.",technology="Caravels and astronomical navigation.",rise="Henry the Navigator's voyages; Vasco da Gama reached India in 1498.",fall="Dutch competition, Brazilian independence (1822), and African wars of independence after 1974.",legacy="The Portuguese language and Goan culture."),
"british-empire":dict(government="Colonies ranged from self-governing dominions to crown colonies and princely states, overseen from London.",architecture="Colonial capitals such as New Delhi and Indo-Saracenic railway stations.",technology="Steamships, railways and the telegraph.",rise="Atlantic colonies, East India Company conquests in India, and industrial and naval power.",fall="World wars, independence movements (India 1947), and decolonisation by the 1960s.",legacy="English as a world language and the Commonwealth, but also exploitation, famines and partitions."),
"russian-empire":dict(government="An autocratic tsar ruled with the nobility and Orthodox Church; serfdom lasted until 1861.",architecture="St Petersburg, Peter the Great's 'window to the West'.",technology="Trans-Siberian railway.",rise="Peter the Great proclaimed the empire in 1721 and it expanded across Siberia to the Pacific.",fall="Defeat by Japan (1905), World War I losses, and the 1917 revolutions.",legacy="Tolstoy, Dostoevsky, Tchaikovsky."),
"soviet-union":dict(government="A one-party Communist state with a centrally planned economy.",architecture="Stalinist 'Seven Sisters' skyscrapers.",technology="Sputnik (1957), Gagarin (1961) and nuclear weapons.",rise="The Bolshevik revolution (1917) and the civil war.",fall="Economic stagnation, Afghanistan, nationalism and Gorbachev's reforms led to dissolution in 1991.",legacy="The defeat of Nazism at huge cost; Cold War; Stalin's terror and famines."),
}
